# sandboxed claude

run claude inside a container without the need of visual studio code
a local directory is mounted on the container where all the code can reside

claude-sandbox: runs claude
claude-proxy: lets claude access claude api and nothing else by default by using tinyproxy

## Installation

### build containers

podman build -t claude-sandbox claude-sandbox
podman build -t claude-proxy claude-proxy

### create networks
internal networks does not have internet or dns access
this also means that the proxy needs a fixed IP because podman-dns cannot be queried
```
podman network create --internal --subnet 10.89.50.0/24 --disable-dns claude-int
```
external network is only attached to the proxy
```
podman network create claude-ext
````

make sure two networks were added
```
podman network list
```
NETWORK ID    NAME        DRIVER
ID1           claude-ext  bridge
ID2           claude-int  bridge
ID3           podman      bridge

use inspect to make sure that dns is disabled on claude-int
```
podman network inspect claude-int | grep dns_enabled
```

### create claude folders - here you can put all files claude should have access to
mkdir ~/claude-sandbox/workspace #holds code
mkdir ~/claude-sandbox/config    #holds session,login-info

### set alias for claude to use container
proxy ip must match the ip in the following run command
```
alias claude='podman run -it --rm --userns=keep-id --name=claude \
  --network claude-int \
  -e http_proxy=http://10.89.50.254:8888 \
  -e https_proxy=http://10.89.50.254:8888 \
  -e CLAUDE_CODE_DISABLE_NONESSENTIAL_TRAFFIC=1 \
  -v ~/claude-sandbox/workspace:/workspace \
  -v ~/claude-sandbox/config:/home/dev/.claude \
  claude-sandbox claude'
```

### run containers manually
claude-proxy will be attached to both internal and external network

```
podman run -it --rm --network claude-int:ip=10.89.50.254 --network claude-ext --name claude-proxy localhost/claude-proxy
```
now you can use the alias claude, login and use the workspace folder
